The introduction of heteroatoms and functional groups in $\mathrm{g-C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ generally has great advantages in enhancing the photocatalytic performance. In this work, the heteroatoms $(Z\mathrm{n}\mathrm{+}\mathrm{C})$ and cyano $(-C{\equiv}\mathrm{N})$ group co-decorated porous $\mathrm{g-C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ nanosheets (DCNNS) photocatalysts were successfully synthesized through direct calcination of the mixed urea and metal-organic frameworks. The optimized DCNNS displayed a maximum $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ evolution rate of ${\sim}484.09~\mathrm{\upmumol/h}$ with a quantum efficiency of ${\sim}3.43\%$ at $420\mathrm{nm}$ , and the photocatalytic U(VI) reduction activity was improved by ${\sim}6.09$ times. The enhanced photocatalytic performance could be ascribed to following benefits: (1) the modified DCNNS shared the two-dimensional layered structure of $\mathrm{g-C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ , and the massive nanopores in the nanosheets provided more reaction sites and diffusion channels for accelerated mass transfer; (2) the formation of cyano group greatly broadened the light response range and also acted as strong electron-withdrawing group for improving the carrier separation rate; (3) heteroatoms doping modulated the band gap, increased the electric conductivity, promoted the carrier separation and transport, and prolonged the electron lifetime to enhance photocatalytic performance. This work suggested that the heteroatoms and functional groups co-decoration could significantly improve the performance of $\mathrm{g-C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ -based photocatalysts and hold great potential to be further explored for energy and environmental applications.
## 1. Introduction
The enormous and increasing consumption of fossil energy has driven humanity to seek new energy sources with lower carbon. Hydrogen energy and nuclear energy are two types of carbon-free energy source and are capable of meeting the growing energy demand. The fast development of nuclear energy surely leads to increasing demands for uranium, which is the key element in nuclear fuels. Therefore, reduction of soluble U(VI) to insoluble U(IV) oxide has been proposed as an important approach to U(VI) extraction from radioactive contamination for further recovery as a fuel.[1-3] Compared to other reduction methods, photocatalytic U(VI) reduction (PUR) has more prospects because it does not produce additional harmful chemicals and is less costly.[4, 5] Meanwhile, the photocatalytic $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ evolution (PHE) was one of the most promising approaches for the production of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ from water.[6-8] Therefore, developing low-cost and environmental-friendly photocatalysts is highly desired in the photocatalytic field.
Graphitic carbon nitride $(\mathrm{g-C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4})$ , a novel metal-free semiconductor, was a promising visible light photocatalyst because of its relatively narrow band gap $(\sim2.7\mathrm{eV})$ , well-suited band positions and high stability.[9-14] However, the practical application of pristine $\mathrm{g-C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ in photocatalysis also faced challenges due to its inherent disadvantages.[15-17] Researchers have made significant efforts to enhance the photocatalytic efficiency of $\mathrm{g-C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ resorting to various strategies, such as nanostructure and defect engineering, heteroatoms doping, heterostructure construction or being decorated with co-catalyst.[18-29]
Heteroatoms doping was a promising and effective strategy for enhancing the photocatalytic activity of $\mathrm{g-C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ because it could simultaneously regulate the band gap edges, the optical response range and conductivity.[30] For example, Dong et al. proposed that carbon self-doping could reduce the band gap and boost the electrical conductivity of $\mathrm{g-C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ through calcinations with melamine pre-treated with absolute ethanol.[31] Tonda et al. observed that Fe-doped $\mathrm{g-C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ nanosheets exhibited red shift and enhanced visible light response ability.[32] In fact, multi-element doping of $\mathrm{g-C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}.$ , such as $\mathrm{P+}\mathrm{S}$ co-doping, $F e{+}C$ co-doping and $\mathrm{S^{+P+O}}$ tri-doping have been shown to afford more excellent photocatalytic activity and unusual physiochemical properties in comparison with single element doping.[33-35] However, few studies have been conducted on the metal and nonmetal co-doped $\mathrm{g-C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ for PHE and U(VI) reduction. In particular, most researches on U(VI) reduction were focused on nonmetal modified $\mathrm{g-C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ .
Rational modification by functional groups ( $\left.-\mathrm{N}\mathrm{H}_{2}\right.$ , -OH and $\partial_{\mathbf{\tau}}-\mathrm{C}\equiv\mathbf{N}$ ) was regarded as one of strategies to modulate the activity of $\mathrm{g-C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ .[36] These functional groups could improve light absorption ability, modulation band structure, and act as the efficient electron-consuming capture agent.[37-40] For example, Yu et al. grafted hydroxyls on $\mathrm{g-C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ through a hydrothermal process.[12] Such surface hydroxylation allowed $\mathrm{g-C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ to realize local spatial charge separation, accounting for the excellent charge transfer. Zhang and coworkers prepared atomically-thin porous $\mathrm{g-C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ nanosheets with oxygen-containing and amino group through a two-step thermal treatment process.[41] This modification increased the separation and transfer rate of photogenerated electron-hole pairs and provided a large number of reaction sites for surface reactions. However, most functional group modification methods generally required additional chemical reagents or post-treatment, increasing the complexity of the preparation process. Moreover, the constructed nanosheets structure modulated the migration distance of photogenerated carriers in the vertical-plane direction while the migration distance in-plane direction was still very long, resulting in ineffective suppression of photogenerated electron-hole recombination.[42] The candidate strategy was to cut the $\mathrm{g-C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ nanosheets into interconnected porous nanosheets with abundant in-plane edges, thereby further shortening the migration distance direction of photogenerated carriers in the plane and promoting the penetration of $\mathrm{H^{+}}$ into the photocatalyst to generate $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ . Nonetheless, most tailored nanosheets strategies involve drawbacks of rigorous post-processing, operational complexity, and the use of hazardous chemical reagents. It is meaningful and intriguing to develop a simple, green, and no-template new strategy for synthesizing porous $\mathrm{g-C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ nanosheets with interconnected porous.
Based on the above considerations, it is conceivable that $\mathrm{g-C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ with the following three characteristics may enhance its photocatalytic performance: (i) cyano $\left(-C{\equiv}\mathrm{N}\right)$ group acts as a strong electron withdrawing group to accelerate the separation rate of photogenerated carriers, (ii) heteroatoms binary doping alter the band structure, improve the electric conductivity and expand light response range, and (iii) a porous structure with high surface area could provide much more reaction sites. So far, the simultaneous combination cyano ( $\partial_{\mathbf{\lambda}}\mathrm{C}\equiv\mathbf{N}_{\mathbf{\lambda}}$ ) group, heteroatoms binary doping and 2 dimensional (2D) nanosheets with interconnected porous are still scarce.
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), constructed from metal ions and organic ligands, were emerging as promising templates and/or precursors to prepare functional nanomaterials via thermolysis.[43] Herein, we present a facile method to synthesize both heteroatoms $(Z\mathrm{n}{+}C)$ and cyano ( $\therefore C=N)$ ) group co-decorated porous $\mathrm{g-C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ nanosheets through direct calcination of the mixed urea and Zn-MOFs as single precursors. The obtained photocatalysts were characterized, and their photocatalytic $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ evolution activity for evolution and U(VI) reduction were evaluated. Our work provided a facile strategy to achieve functional groups and heteroatoms co-decorated $\mathrm{g-C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ using MOFs as a single precursor.
## 2. Experimental section
## 2.1 Synthesis of pristine g-C3N4 nanosheets (CNNS)
Generally, $\boldsymbol{15}\mathrm{\textrm{g}}$ of urea was heated at $550^{\mathrm{{\circ}C}}$ in an alumina crucible with a cover for $2\mathrm{~h~}$ $(2.3~\mathrm{{^{o}C}~m i n^{-1}})$ ) in a muffle furnace. The resultant yellow product was collected for further use.
## 2.2 Synthesis of Zn-MOFs precursor
Typically, zinc nitrate $(\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})$ and hexamethylenetetramine (HMTA) with molar ratio of 5:1 were dissolved in ethanol, respectively. Then, the $Z_{\mathrm{n}}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ solution was added to the HMTA solution. Afterwards, the precipitates were obtained after stirring for $30~\mathrm{min}$ , centrifugation and washed repeatedly with ethanol, and dried at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ .
2.3 Synthesis of heteroatoms $(Z n{+}C)$ and cyano (- $.C{\equiv}N)$ group co-decorated $g{-}C_{3}N_{4}$ nanosheets (DCNNS)
A series of DCNNS photocatalysts were prepared through direct calcination of urea and Zn-MOFs precursor. Typically, $\boldsymbol{15}\mathrm{g}$ of urea was ground with Zn-MOFs precursor, and then the solid mixtures were calcined at $550~\mathrm{{^\circC}}$ for $^{4\mathrm{~h~}}$ $(2.3~\mathrm{^{o}C~m i n^{-1}})$ in a muffle furnace. The obtained samples were ground for fine powders and marked as DCNNSx (where $\mathbf{x}=0.02$ , 0.05, 0.1, 0.2 and 0.3, and x corresponds to the mass (g) of Zn-MOFs precursor used).
## 2.4. Characterization
The morphologies were studied by scanning electron microscope (SEM, JSM-7500) and transmission electron microscope (TEM, JEOL-2100F). The crystal structures were investigated by X-ray diffraction (XRD, Bruker D8 Advanced) equipped with a Cu Kα radiation. The chemical states were learned by X-ray photoelectron spectroscope (XPS, Thermo Fisher ESCALAB 250Xi). Fourier transform infrared (FTIR) spectra were recorded on a Nicolet-Nexus 670 infrared spectroscopy. Nitrogen adsorption/desorption measurements were conducted with an Autosorb- $\cdot\mathrm{IQ}_{2}$ (Quantachrome Corporation) through nitrogen sorption. The specific surface areas of the samples were calculated using the Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) method. Photoluminescence (PL) data were collected on a Hitachi F4600 Luminescence Spectrometer. UV-vis diffuse reflectance spectra (DRS) were performed using a Shimadzu UV-3600 UV-vis spectrophotometer. Solid electron spin resonance (ESR) signals of samples were investigated by a Bruker model A300 spectrometer at room temperature.
## 2.5 Photocatalytic $H_{2}$ evolution
Typically, photocatalysts $\mathrm{10~mg)}$ were suspended in triethanolamine (TEOA)
aqueous solution (10 vol. $\%$ ). ${\sim}3~\mathrm{wt\%}$ Pt was loaded on the photocatalysts by an in situ photo-deposition method using $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{PtCl}_{6}$ . The suspension was vacuumed thoroughly, and then exposed to a 300 W Xenon lamp ((PLS-SXE, Perfectlight, China)) equipped with a UV cutoff filter $\lambda\ge420\mathrm{nm})$ . The amount of evolved $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ was determined by an online gas chromatography using argon as the carrier gas.
## 2.6 Photocatalytic U(VI) reduction
Typically, photocatalysts ( $\mathrm{100~mg)}$ were added in $\mathrm{UO}_{2}{}^{2+}$ solution $\mathrm{0.12~mM}$ , 200 mL, containing $2.5~\mathrm{mL}$ methanol as sacrificial agent). A 300 W Xeon-lamp with a cutoff $(\lambda\ge420\mathrm{nm})$ ) was used as the light source. And then, the solution was stirred in the anaerobic-dark environment for $2\mathrm{~h~}$ to achieve adsorption/desorption equilibrium. At certain time intervals, $4~\mathrm{mL}$ of the suspension was analyzed to remove the photocatalyst by centrifugation. The U(VI) concentrations were detected by Shimadzu UV-2600 UV-vis spectrophotometer at 650 nm wavelength.
## 3. Results and discussion
XRD measurements were used to confirm the phase structures of the samples. It is shown in Figure 1A that the pristine CNNS exhibited two main characteristic diffraction peaks at ${\sim}13.04^{\mathrm{o}}$ and ${\sim}27.45^{\mathrm{o}}$ , which can be assigned to the (100) and (002) diffraction planes of $\mathrm{g-C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ .[44] The strong peak at ${\sim}27.45^{\mathrm{o}}$ could be ascribed to the interlayer stacking of the aromatic system, while the weak peak at ${\sim}13.04^{\mathrm{o}}$ could be indexed to in-planar-striazine structural packing motif composed of tris-s-triazine units.[11] XRD results suggested well crystalline framework structure of Zn-MOFs (Figure S1), which was consistent with previously determined MOFs.[45] The characteristic peaks of DCNNS-x gradually became broader and weaker with the increasing amount of Zn-MOFs, indicating the decrease of crystallinity or abundant defects. These differences implied that the original block structure has been transformed into a porous structure. Meanwhile, no characteristic peaks of Zn species were observed, revealing that the incorporation of Zn did not produce a new crystalline phase.
The FTIR spectra of pristine CNNS and DCNNS-x are shown in Figure 1B. The peaks at ${\sim}3000{\cdot}3500~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ were correlated to -NH and -OH stretching vibrations. The characteristic bands in the range of $\sim1200{-}1650\ \mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ and at ${\sim}810~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ were observed, corresponding to the stretching modes of C-N heterocyclic and the vibrational mode of s-triazine units, respectively.[30] Moreover, a new peak at ${\sim}2172\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ appeared, which belonged to the cyano $\scriptstyle\mathbf{\underline{{\bar{\mathbf{\Lambda}}}}}\mathbf{C}\equiv\mathbf{N})$ group asymmetric stretching vibration.[44, 46, 47] The cyano ( $\mathbf{\partial}_{-C}\equiv\mathbf{N}$ groups, the electron pairs in which were strongly biased to N, were regarded as strong electron acceptors, thereby contributing to the improvement of the photocatalytic performance.
$\Nu_{2}$ adsorption/desorption analysis of pristine CNNS and DCNNS-0.1 was carried out to evaluate their surface area and porosity. As depicted in Figure 1C and S2, all samples have typical hysteresis loops belonging to type IV isotherms, indicating the multilayer adsorption process of photocatalysts. The surface area of the pristine CNNS, DCNNS-0.02, DCNNS-0.05, DCNNS-0.1, DCNNS-0.2 and DCNNS-0.3 were detected as ${\sim}68.54\$ , ${\sim}73.75$ , ${\sim}85.07$ , \~115.44, ${\sim}84.41$ and ${\sim}65.23~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ , respectively. Namely, on increasing the Zn-MOF content from 0.02 to 0.1, the surface area of
DCNNS increases obviously, and then the surface area decreased with further increase of Zn-MOF content from 0.1 to 0.3. A larger surface area could provide more illumination area and more potential reaction sites for $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ evolution. The insert of Figure 1C and S2 were the pore size distribution of samples, a similar pore-size (radius) distribution in the region of ${\sim}0.5{-}30~\mathrm{nm}$ was presented in samples, and a gradually increased peak at $0.5\mathrm{-}10\mathrm{nm}$ could be clearly observed from pristine CNNS to DCNNS0.1, manifesting more micropores and mesopores endowed by the Zn-MOF modify. As shown in Table S1, in comparison to pristine CNNS, the average pore sizes of the DCNNS-0.1 have reduced, suggestive of the generation of massive micropores and mesopores. Especially, the DCNNS-0.1 possessed a larger pore volume and pore structure after Zn-MOFs modification, which could facilitate reactants and sacrificial agents across the nanosheets unimpededly during the photocatalytic reactions. The surface area of Zn-MOF was ${\sim}3.93\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ , and pore size analyses (the inset in Figure S3) showed that most of the pores fall into the size range of $^{\sim1}$ to $\sim50\mathrm{nm}$ . The pore volume was ${\sim}0.0085\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g}.$
The unpaired electrons in pristine CNNS and DCNNS-0.1 were studied using EPR technology. In Figure 1D, the Lorentzian line centered at $\mathbf{g}=-2.002$ for pristine CNNS and DCNNS-0.1, were assigned to unpaired electrons in $\mathrm{g-C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ . Compared to the pristine CNNS, the single intensity of DCNNS-0.1 was significantly enhanced, which should be attributed to the redistribution of $\pi$ -electrons and the introduction of cyano (- $\mathrm{C}\equiv\mathrm{N}$ ) group defects in DCNNS-0.1.[21] The defects could narrow the band gap to enhance light absorption and improve the photogenerated charge carrier separation.
XPS was carried out to characterize the chemical composition of pristine CNNS and DCNNS-0.1. The survey scan spectra in Figure 2A revealed the existence of C and N elements in pristine CNNS, while DCNNS-0.1 contain Zn element, besides C and N elements, indicating that the Zn element has been doped into $\mathrm{g-C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ successfully. The C 1s spectra of pristine CNNS (Figure 2B) displayed three peaks at \~284.86, \~286.51 and ${\sim}288.31\ \mathrm{eV}$ , associated with graphitic carbon, carbon $\mathrm{{C-NH}_{\mathrm{{x}}}}$ species and $\displaystyle\mathbf{sp}^{2}$ hybridized C atom attached to N inside the triazine ring $\scriptstyle(\mathrm{N-C=N})$ , respectively.[8, 26] Notably, the signal of the peak at ${\sim}286.51\mathrm{eV}$ of DCNNS-0.1 was intensified compared to pristine CNNS, which could be taken as additional evidence for the generated cyano $(-C{\equiv}\mathrm{N})$ group due to the similar binding energy with $\mathrm{{C-NH_{x}}}$ . Meanwhile, the proportion of peak area at ${\sim}284.86\mathrm{eV}$ in DCNNS-0.1 increased compared to that of pristine CNNS $(\sim10.58\%$ vs. ${\sim}2.74\%$ ), while the proportion of peak area at ${\sim}288.31~\mathrm{eV}$ in DCNNS0.1 decreased compared to that of pristine CNNS ( ${\sim}80.89\%$ vs. ${\sim}69.24\%$ ), clearly demonstrating that the formation of cyano ( $\scriptstyle\mathbf{\bar{\Pi}}-\mathbf{C}\equiv\mathbf{N})$ group might be due to the cleavage of ${\mathrm{N-C}}{\mathrm{=N}}$ heterocycles. As shown in Figure 2C, the N 1s spectra of pristine CNNS could be resolved into four different peaks at ${\sim}398.88\$ , ${\sim}400.48\$ , ${\sim}401.77\$ and ${\sim}404.56\$ eV, which were attributed to the bi-coordinated N ( $\mathrm{C-N=C)}$ , tricoordinated N $\left(\mathrm{N}{-}(\mathrm{C})_{3}\right)$ , amino N $\mathrm{(C-NH_{x})}$ ) and charging effects in the cyano group, respectively.[15] The binding peaks at \~1045.46 and ${\sim}1022.47\ \mathrm{eV}$ , respectively, were designated as $\mathrm{Zn}2\mathsf{p}_{1/2}$ and $Z{\textnormal{n}}2{\textnormal{p}}_{3/2}$ , confirming the presence of Zn element on the DCNNS-0.1 surface, as shown in Figure 2D.[48] Compared to the pristine CNNS, the atomic ratio of C/N in DCNNS-0.1 increased from ${\sim}0.64$ to ${\sim}1.13$ , confirming the successful incorporation of carbon into $\mathrm{g-C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ matrix. The carbon doping would optimize the aromatic $\pi$ conjugated system in $\mathrm{g-C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ to achieve better photogenerated electron-hole pair separation, and also improved conductivity and photogenerated carrier mobility. The XPS results indicated the occurrence of $Z_{\mathrm{{n+C}}}$ codoping in the $\mathrm{g-C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ framework.
SEM and TEM were used to observe morphological features of as-prepared samples. The Zn-MOFs precursor presented a 2D nanosheets structure with the size ranging from tens of nanometers to micrometers (Figure 3A). From the side view, the thickness of nanosheets was ${\sim}10~\mathrm{nm}$ . The pristine CNNS consisted of thick crimped nanosheets and some aggregates with a size of several micrometers appeared, as shown in Figure 3B. However, DCNNS-0.1 displayed a curled nanosheets porous worm-like morphology and appeared to be more loosely connected; this scenario was significantly different from the large and flat sheet structure of pristine CNNS sample (Figure 3C). The pristine CNNS consisted of smooth nanosheets and some loose aggregates with a size of several micrometers appeared (Figure 3D). It is interesting that the DCNNS-0.1 exhibited strikingly different morphology. Further observation proved the existence of abundant in-plane nanopores of a few nanometers as compared to pristine CNNS, demonstrating the formation of porous carbon nitride sheets (Figure 3E and F). Therefore, we suspected that the introduction of $Z\mathbf{n}$ -MOFs during calcination could etch the surface of pristine CNNS to produce porous $\mathrm{g-C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ nanosheets. These nanopores should be originated from the burst of soft bubbles which were created by $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ gases during the Zn-MOFs condensation.[49, 50] No clear lattice fringes were found in HRTEM image (Figure 3G), indicating the low crystallinity of DCNNS.
Additionally, no obvious nanocrystallines could be detected in the SEM and TEM images, further confirming that no other zinc-based species were formed in the DCNNS. This fact was in good agreement with the XRD results. A typical HAAD-STEM images showed uniform dispersion of the C, N and Zn elements in the whole nanosheets structure (Figure 3H-J), further demonstrating the uniform dispersion heteroatoms $(Z\mathrm{n+C})$ doping.
The UV-vis DRS were measured to elucidate the light absorption property of photocatalysts. As shown in Figure 4A, pristine CNNS had the absorption edge at ${\sim}447\mathrm{nm}$ , while the absorption peaks of DCNNS-x were significantly stronger and broader, along with the red shift of absorption edges from ${\sim}490$ to ${\sim}960~\mathrm{nm}$ . The increase of both red shift and absorption intensity implied that the samples were more efficient in utilizing sunlight and photogenerated charge carriers. The band gap $\mathrm{(E_{g})}$ was determined from $(\mathrm{ahv})^{1/2}$ versus hν plots, as shown in Figure 4B. The band gaps for pristine CNNS and DCNNS-0.1 were found to be ${\sim}2.77$ and ${\sim}2.37\mathrm{eV}$ , respectively. The DCNNS-0.1 possessed a narrower band gap than pristine CNNS, which might be due to the synergistic effect of heteroatoms $(Z\mathrm{n}\mathrm{+}\mathrm{C})$ doping and cyano $\therefore C=N)$ group. The valence band (VB) XPS technique was used to obtain the VB position (Figure 4C). The VB potential of pristine CNNS and DCNNS-0.1 were estimated to be ${\sim}1.78$ and ${\sim}2.05\mathrm{V}$ , respectively. According to the band gap energy estimated from UV-vis DRS results, the conduction band (CB) potential of pristine CNNS and DCNNS-0.1 were calculated to be $\mathord{\sim}-0.99$ and $\mathrm{\sim}-0.32\mathrm{eV}$ , respectively. Thus, the CB and VB positions were down-shifted in DCNNS-0.1 in comparison with pristine CNNS, and the detailed band structure diagram is illustrated in Figure 4D.
The PHE performance of the photocatalysts was comparatively evaluated, and the results are presented in Figure 5A. The contents of Zn-MOFssignificantly impacted the PHE of DCNNS-x samples. All of the DCNNS-x samples exhibited an enhanced PHE activity compared to pristine CNNS, achieving a maximum for DCNNS-0.1. Doping could reduce the recombination probability of photogenerated charge carriers and prolong photogenerated electrons lifetime by introducing impurity levels into the forbidden band.[51] However, excessive doping could also increase recombination centers for photogenerated electron-hole pairs. The corresponding PHE rates of pristine CNNS and DCNNS- $\mathbf{\sigma}\cdot\mathbf{x}$ photocatalysts are presented in Figure 5B. Among them, DCNNS-0.1 exhibited a highest PHE rate of ${\sim}484.09~\mathrm{\upmumol/h}$ , which was ${\sim}3.61$ times higher than that of pristine CNNS $(\small{\sim}134.18~\upmu\mathrm{mol/h})$ . The apparent quantum efficiency (AQE) for PHE of DCNNS-0.1 was investigated under various monochromatic light irradiation conditions as shown in Figure 5C. It can be seen that a high AQE of ${\sim}3.43\%$ was achieved for DCNNS-0.1 under irradiation at $420\mathrm{nm}$ . Moreover, the tendency of AQE curve matched well with the UV-vis DRS of DCNNS-0.1, indicating that the PHE reaction was induced by light absorption of $\mathrm{g-C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ . To evaluate the stabilities of DCNNS-0.1, the cycling runs for hydrogen evolution are shown in Figure 5D. The photocatalytic activity of DCNNS-0.1 did not decrease significantly even after five cycle tests, suggesting its high stability. This method was rather general and could be also used to prepare other heteroatoms and cyano group co-decorated $\mathrm{g-C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ nanosheets using different metal-based MOF precursor, such as Cu-MOF, Co-MOF, etc. Photocatalytic $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ production activities over different co-decorated photocatalysts were also evaluated. As shown in Figure S4, all of co-decorated photocatalysts exhibited considerably enhanced PHE activities. Obviously, DCNNS have the greatest photocatalytic activity. Although the exact reason for this phenomenon was still unclear, it might be attributed to the fact that the Zn-MOF precursor had 2D nanosheets structure, while the Co-MOF and Cu-MOF precursors were bulk structures (Figure S5). Nanosheet structure precursors were more conducive to the formation of uniform doping.
Furthermore, the PUR under visible light irradiation were carried out to further investigate the underlying environmental application value. As exhibited in Figure 6A, the PUR rate could be neglected in the absence of photocatalyst and the reduction efficiency of U(VI) over the pristine CNNS was only ${\sim}27.4\%$ within 3 h. However, the DCNNS-x displayed higher photocatalytic efficiencies than that of pristine CNNS, which implied that the heteroatoms $(Z\boldsymbol{\mathrm{n}}{+}C)$ doping and cyano ( $\mathrm{\partial-C\equivN)}$ group could promoted the separation and migration of photogenerated electron-hole pairs, improved the absorption of visible light, and enhanced the photocatalytic activity. Moreover, the reduction kinetics were evaluated by the pseudo-first-order kinetics equation and shown in Figure 6B. The kinetics rate constant k values were \~0.1605, ${\sim}0.2559$ , ${\sim}0.5783$ and ${\sim}0.4239\ \mathrm{h}^{-1}$ with increasing the amount of Zn-MOFs precursor. The photocatalytic efficiency of the optimum DCNNS-0.1 was ${\sim}6.09$ times higher than that of pristine gC3N4 $(0.0949\mathrm{h^{-1}}$ ). As shown in Figure 6C, the rate of reduction of U(VI) by DCNNS0.1 was not significantly deactivated after four cycles, indicating that DCNNS-0.1 had excellent photocatalytic stability. To identify the distribution of the oxidation state of the U element, the high-resolution spectra of U 4f was obtained by XPS analysis (Figure 6D). Two peaks at ${\sim}390.91\$ and ${\sim}379.84\mathrm{eV}$ corresponded to the U $4\mathrm{f}_{5/2}$ and U $4\mathrm{f}_{7/2}$ of U(IV), respectively, clearly showing the photoreduction of U(VI).[2] However, XPS results indicated that most of uranium element was still in the form of U(VI), probably because U(IV) was easily reoxidized by photogenerated holes or other free radicals, returning to the initial state U(VI).
The photogenerated charge separation and transfer performance were evaluated by the transient photocurrent response (TPR) and electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) (Figure 7). The TPR results showed that the photocurrent density of DCNNS-0.1 was clearly larger than that of pristine CNNS, which demonstrated that the separation and transfer efficiency of photogenerated electro-hole pairs was improved in DCNNS0.1 (Figure 7A). As shown in Figure 7B, DCNNS-0.1 exhibited much smaller EIS Nyquist plot semicircle compared to that of pristine CNNS, indicating that the charge transfer resistance of DCNNS-0.1 was obviously decreased. This fact also implied that the photogenerated charge carriers of DCNNS-0.1 enjoyed higher separation efficiency, which was in accord with the TPR results. Both TPR and EIS results proved the incorporation of heteroatoms $(Z\mathrm{n}\mathrm{+}\mathrm{C})$ doping and cyano ( $\scriptstyle-C\equiv\mathrm{N})$ group could facilitate the charge carrier separation and transfer in photocatalysts. Meanwhile, the porous structure could also reduce the transport distance of the photogenerated charge carrier. PL was further applied to further explore the photogenerated charge carrier recombination process. Figure 7C showed the room temperature PL spectra of pristine
CNNS and DCNNS-0.1 under an excitation of $365\mathrm{nm}$ . Predictably, the PL intensity of DCNNS-0.1 was dramatically lower than that of pristine CNNS, which indicated that the recombination of electrons and hole pairs could be effectively reduced by introducing the heteroatoms $(Z\boldsymbol{\mathrm{n}}{+}C)$ doping and cyano $\scriptstyle\mathbf{\underline{{\bar{\mathbf{\Lambda}}}}}\mathbf{C}\equiv\mathbf{N})$ group into $\mathrm{g-C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ . Moreover, the PL peak for DCNNS-0.1 showed a blue-shift in contrast to pristine CNNS, which was in accordance with the bandgap blue-shift as shown in the Figure 4A. Time-resolved PL decay spectroscopy (Figure 7D) illustrated that the average lifetime of DCNNS-0.1 obviously increased to ${\sim}4.03$ ns compared to pristine CNNS (\~3.62 ns). The results suggested that a new nonraditive decay pathway might be built, contributing to the prolonged lifetime of photogenerated charge carriers and highly efficient photogenerated electron-hole pairs separation. Overall, the above electrochemical and PL results clearly showed that the heteroatoms $(Z\mathrm{n}\mathrm{+}\mathrm{C})$ and cyano $(-C{\equiv}\mathrm{N})$ ) group promoted the separation efficiency of electrons-hole pairs, reduced the photocurrent impedance of the material surface, and prolonged the lifetime of photogenerated carriers, thus enhanced reduction effect of U(VI).
Based on above results, the tentative mechanisms of photocatalytic process on the DCNNS are depicted in Figure 8. The DCNNS could yield many photogenerated electron-hole pairs under the visible-light illumination. These electrons rapidly injected into the CB and then transferred to the surface. For PHE, these electrons accumulated on the surface of Pt nanoparticles were used for the reduction of $\mathrm{H^{+}}$ to $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ . The remained holes were consumed by the sacrificial electron donor of TEOA. For PUR, the photogenerated electrons on the surface of DCNNS could transfer to U(VI), which resulted in the reduction and elimination of U(VI).
## 4. Conclusions
In summary, we presented a facile strategy to synthesize both cyano ( $\therefore C\equiv\mathrm{N})$ group and heteroatoms co-decorated porous $\mathrm{g-C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ nanosheets through a direct calcination of the mixed urea and Zn-MOFs as single precursors. Their BET surface areas were calculated to be ${\sim}73.81~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ for pristine CNNS and ${\sim}115.44~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ for DCNNS-0.1, respectively. A larger surface area could provide more illumination area and more potential reaction sites for PHE and PUR. The ESR results indicated that the presence of a large number of unpaired electrons in DCNNS was due to the formation of cyano $(-C{\equiv}\mathrm{N})$ group. The UV-vis DRS absorption peaks of DCNNS were significantly stronger and broader, and the absorption edges were red shifted from ${\sim}490$ to ${\sim}960\mathrm{nm}$ . The unique dual co-decorated strategy provided faster photogenerated electron-hole separation rates and extended the lifetime of photogenerated carriers, as evidenced by electrochemical analysis and PL/TRPL spectrum. Together with these advantages, the optimum DCNNS-0.1 exhibited a highest hydrogen evolution rate of ${\sim}484.09~\mathrm{\upmumol/h}$ , which was ${\sim}3.61$ times higher than that of pristine CNNS. A high AQE of ${\sim}3.43\%$ was achieved for DCNNS-0.1 under irradiation at $420\mathrm{nm}$ . Meanwhile, the DCNNS presented highly efficient PUR activity, was ${\sim}6.09$ times than that of pristine CNNS. Our results suggested that the functional groups and heteroatoms co-decorated as a simple modification technology could significantly improve the $\mathrm{g-C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ -based photocatalysts performance and hold a great potential to be further explored for energy and environmental applications.
